---
name: Art Generator
description: End-to-end illustration generation for MyPhonicsBooks. Treats page-to-page consistency as an asset-and-state management problem — a frozen Book World Pack of visual references (style, characters, settings, objects), a continuity manifest, sequential continuity-chained scene generation, and hard-fail QC gates. Engine: Vertex AI Gemini image models (multi-reference injection).
---

# Art Generator — MyPhonicsBooks (v2, continuity-first)

Produce story illustrations in which characters, settings, objects, style and
story state remain consistent from page to page.

Treat consistency as an asset-and-state management problem. Repeated prose is
never a substitute for an approved visual reference.

---

## Non-negotiable rules

- Finalise the story before generating art.
- Build and approve the complete Book World Pack before generating pages.
- Assign a stable ID to every recurring character, setting and object.
- Inject every relevant approved reference into every scene.
- Generate continuity-dependent scenes sequentially. Do not generate them in
  parallel.
- Never replace an approved reference during a book run.
- Edit from an approved image when only a small visual change is needed.
- Fail closed: never deliver an image with an unresolved hard failure.
- Preserve the MyPhonicsBooks eye rule: eyes are simple solid black ovals or
  dots with no white, shine, reflection, iris or highlight.
- Use modest, child-appropriate clothing. Do not show bare thighs, revealing
  clothing, swimwear or inappropriate imagery. (No shorts — use joggers,
  trousers or leggings.)
- Do not include visible words, letters, numbers, watermarks or signatures in
  final illustrations. Never invent lettering on signs, packaging or clothing.
- Do not claim perfect consistency. Apply the reference, state and validation
  gates below to make drift detectable and repairable.
- For exact unchanged elements, reuse their pixels or locked transparent
  layer; never ask the model to redraw them.
- Settings need architecture, not a nationality: define every setting by
  drawable, countable features (e.g. brick terraces, slate roofs, chimney
  pots, cobbles, bunting) in EVERY prompt including close-ups, and always
  name the ground surface. "British street" or "cosy kitchen" alone is a
  placeless prompt and is forbidden.

## Required inputs

Do not begin image generation until these inputs exist:

- **identity and structure:** book_id, final page text, page count and cover
  brief;
- **formats:** cover and story-page aspect ratios;
- **audience:** reader age, phonics level and personalisation mode (template
  or uploaded_child);
- **visual controls:** character specification or roster (Appendix A),
  approved MyPhonicsBooks style reference, brand rules and safeguarding
  rules.

If a required detail is absent, choose only a reversible visual detail and
record it. Never invent a new recurring character, location or plot object.

## Pipeline

Use this order:

```
1.  Parse story
2.  Build continuity manifest
3.  Create visual reference assets
4.  Validate and freeze Book World Pack
5.  Build page-by-page scene plan
6.  Generate scenes by continuity chain
7.  Validate each scene
8.  Repair failed scenes
9.  Validate the complete book as a contact sheet
10. Deliver images, references, manifest and QC report
```

No page generation may start before step 4 passes.

---

## 1. Parse the story into continuity entities

Read all pages together. Extract:

- every character and the pages on which they appear;
- every setting and whether pages show the same physical place;
- every recurring object, including small background items such as plants,
  lamps, cushions, pictures, windows and rugs;
- object state changes, such as closed to open, clean to muddy, empty to
  full, whole to broken, or indoors to carried outside;
- clothing changes that are explicitly required by the story;
- time, weather and lighting changes;
- action, emotion and visible outcome for each page;
- facts that must not appear until a later page.

Separate persistent identity, temporary scene state, camera choice and the one
meaningful story delta from the preceding scene.

## 2. Build the continuity manifest

Create `continuity.json` as the source of truth. Use stable IDs rather than
descriptive names in page plans.

Minimum shape:

```json
{
  "book_id": "L4_B2",
  "style_id": "STYLE-01",
  "characters": {
    "CHAR-01": {
      "reference_files": ["references/CHAR-01-full.png", "references/CHAR-01-face.png"],
      "hard_invariants": ["same face, skin, hair and outfit", "solid black eyes with zero white", "no blush"]
    }
  },
  "settings": {
    "SET-01": {
      "reference_files": ["references/SET-01-master.png", "references/SET-01-reverse.png"],
      "hard_invariants": ["arched window on left wall", "blue sofa on rear wall"]
    }
  },
  "objects": {
    "OBJ-PLANT-01": {
      "reference_file": "references/OBJ-PLANT-01.png",
      "hard_invariants": ["round terracotta pot", "three broad green leaves"]
    }
  },
  "pages": [
    {
      "page": 1,
      "text": "Final story text",
      "setting_id": "SET-01",
      "character_ids": ["CHAR-01"],
      "object_ids": ["OBJ-PLANT-01"],
      "action": "visible present action only",
      "state_before": {},
      "state_after": {},
      "forbidden_future_facts": [],
      "previous_scene_id": null
    }
  ]
}
```

Record exact colours as hex values when colour identity matters. Also retain a
plain-language colour name because image models do not render hex values with
absolute precision.

Do not make the manifest self-contradictory. For example, never specify both
"rosy cheeks" and "no blush". (House ruling: NO blush, NO rosy cheeks, ever —
if a roster entry says otherwise, the no-blush rule wins.)

When a count matters (leaves, stars, buttons, spots), state the count as a
hard invariant AND verify it in QC — image models routinely miscount
("6 stars" → 5 stars is a known recurring failure).

## 3. Create the Book World Pack

The Book World Pack is the frozen set of references used for the entire book.

### 3.1 Style anchor

Use one approved MyPhonicsBooks illustration as STYLE-01. It controls:

- 2D hand-drawn children's book appearance;
- clean black character outlines;
- soft watercolour-textured backgrounds;
- warm, friendly, uncluttered composition;
- soft pastel base colours with controlled bright accents;
- simple rounded forms;
- solid-black eye treatment.

Do not ask a model to copy the reference scene or character. Ask it to
preserve only the art language.

### 3.2 Character references

For each recurring character, create:

- a full-body neutral reference;
- a close face reference;
- an optional turnaround or expression sheet when several angles or strong
  emotions are required.

Create references against a plain background. Show the complete body, both
hands and both feet. Do not place props in the character reference.

For a template character, include exact garments, colour names and hex
values, skin tone (use the hex table in Appendix B — never vague words like
"dark brown skin"), hair shape, age, height and distinctive features.

For an uploaded child:

- use the uploaded image only for the authorised book;
- preserve recognisable facial features without making the result
  photorealistic;
- convert the child into the approved MyPhonicsBooks illustration style;
- never expose private image URLs in logs or prompts;
- use private or short-lived signed storage;
- delete temporary uploads according to the product privacy policy.

Reject a character reference if the eyes contain any white pixels or
highlights that read as eye shine.

### 3.3 Setting references

Create a clean setting master for every recurring physical location before
adding characters.

The setting master must fix:

- room or landscape geometry;
- door and window count and positions;
- large furniture and permanent features;
- recurring background objects;
- colour palette;
- time of day, weather and base lighting;
- clear left, centre and right landmarks;
- the named ground surface (grass, cobbles, tiles, sand — never unstated).

If later pages use a reverse angle, create `SET-xx-reverse.png` by editing or
deriving it from the approved master. Do not generate the reverse view from
text alone.

Do not use "a cosy living room" as a setting definition. Use observable,
countable features and a visual reference.

### 3.4 Object references

Create an isolated reference for every plot-critical object and every
background object whose changing appearance would be noticeable. Keep a
transparent cut-out PNG when the object may be composited unchanged.

Record:

- shape and silhouette;
- material;
- colour;
- pattern;
- number of parts or leaves;
- relative scale;
- allowed story states.

If an object changes state, create approved state references when the change
is visually important, for example `OBJ-BOX-01-closed.png` and
`OBJ-BOX-01-open.png`.

### 3.5 Single-image reference board

Use a labelled composite reference board only when the chosen endpoint accepts
one image. Place the character, setting and objects in separate
non-overlapping panels with strong spacing.

The board is a degraded compatibility method. Prefer a model that accepts
multiple separate reference images because composite boards can cause panel
blending. (The production Vertex Gemini endpoint DOES accept multiple
labelled `inlineData` reference images — use them.)

### 3.6 Freeze gate

Validate all reference assets together. Once approved:

- mark them `locked: true`;
- calculate and store a file hash;
- retain the exact files for the entire run;
- reject accidental replacement or regeneration.

## 4. Build the scene plan

For every page, create a structured scene record before writing a prose
prompt.

The record must answer:

- Who is visible?
- Where exactly are they?
- What are they doing at this moment?
- Which objects are visible and in what state?
- What has changed since the previous scene?
- What must remain unchanged?
- Which future event must not be revealed?
- What framing makes the action immediately understandable to a child?

Keep visual storytelling varied through framing, camera distance and
character position, while keeping world identity fixed.

Do not interpret "match the story" as illustrating every noun in the text.
Illustrate the current page's event without revealing later outcomes.

## 5. Choose the generation mode

### Mode A: Multi-reference scene generation

Use the current production Vertex Gemini image model (see Engine notes) for
scenes containing a recurring character plus a recurring setting or object.

Pass the references as separate ordered inputs:

1. style anchor;
2. each visible character reference;
3. setting master;
4. required recurring object references;
5. previous approved scene when it contains relevant continuity.

Introduce every image with a text label stating its ID and role. Never assume
the model will infer which reference controls which element.

### Mode B: Controlled edit

Use the previous approved scene as the source when the setting and camera
should stay almost identical and only an action, expression or object state
changes. Lock all unchanged pixels. Composite locked object layers where
practical, then edit or inpaint only the smallest required region for pose,
contact and shadows.

The prompt must state:

- what to preserve;
- the single permitted change;
- what must not be added or removed.

Prefer a localised or masked edit when available.

### Mode C: New angle in an existing setting

Use the locked character references, setting master, reverse-angle reference
when available, and relevant object references. Do not derive a major new
camera angle through repeated edits of the previous page; repeated edits
compound drift.

### Continuity ordering

Generate each location's scenes in story order. A page may begin only after
its declared `previous_scene_id` has passed validation.

Independent locations may be queued separately only after their own reference
packs are locked.

Store the model ID, model version when available, request ID, seed when
exposed, reference hashes and final prompt for every output. A seed aids
reproducibility; it does not replace visual references.

## 6. Construct the scene prompt

Use this order:

```
TASK
Create story page [PAGE] for book [BOOK_ID].

REFERENCE ROLES
[STYLE-01] controls art style only.
[CHAR-01] controls the exact character identity, face, hair, outfit and eyes.
[SET-01] controls the exact physical location and permanent background.
[OBJ-PLANT-01] controls the exact plant design.
[SCENE-01] controls continuity from the preceding approved page.

PRESERVE
List the hard invariants copied from the manifest.

SHOW NOW
Describe the current visible action, pose, expression, object state and camera.

ONLY STORY DELTA
State exactly what changed from the previous approved scene.

DO NOT SHOW
List forbidden future events, unwanted extra people or objects, text, eye
highlights, blush, style drift and anatomy errors.

FORMAT
[aspect ratio], full-bleed composition, safe margins for the book template.
No text, words, letters, numbers, watermark or signature.
```

Use positive, concrete descriptions. Refer to entities by stable ID and
repeat their short invariant clauses. Avoid long decorative prose that
competes with continuity instructions.

Do not say only "same character", "same room" or "same plant". Bind each
phrase to its reference ID and list the critical invariants.

## 7. Validate every generated scene

Run a separate vision-quality check before accepting an image. Compare the
candidate with:

- each referenced entity;
- the continuity manifest;
- the previous approved scene;
- the exact page text;
- the MyPhonicsBooks visual rules.

Return structured QC:

```json
{
  "scene_id": "SCENE-02",
  "hard_pass": false,
  "checks": {
    "character_identity": "pass",
    "solid_black_eyes": "pass",
    "setting_geometry": "fail",
    "recurring_objects": "fail",
    "story_action": "pass",
    "temporal_state": "pass",
    "no_text": "pass",
    "style_and_safeguarding": "pass"
  },
  "failures": ["SET-01 window moved", "OBJ-PLANT-01 has five leaves"],
  "repair_mode": "controlled_edit"
}
```

Hard checks require 100% pass:

- character identity and proportions;
- eye rule;
- clothing and modesty;
- entity count;
- plot object identity and state;
- permanent setting geometry;
- action and emotion;
- chronological correctness;
- no text;
- correct aspect ratio and dimensions;
- no malformed hands, limbs or faces;
- age suitability.

Do not average hard failures into an overall score. One hard failure rejects
the image.

## 8. Repair without causing new drift

Choose the smallest repair:

| Failure | Repair |
|---------|--------|
| Small object, expression or colour error | Controlled edit of the failed image |
| Background drift with correct character | Edit the failed image using the setting master |
| Character drift with correct composition | Replace or edit the character using locked character references |
| Object identity drift | Edit using the exact object reference and state |
| Wrong action or premature plot reveal | Regenerate the scene from the full reference set |
| Major composition or anatomy failure | Regenerate the scene from the full reference set |
| Style drift across several pages | Regenerate only affected pages with the same style anchor |

After every repair, rerun every QC check, not only the failed one. A repair
may damage an element that previously passed.

Allow at most three attempts per scene. If it still fails, stop and return
the candidate images, QC evidence and exact unresolved failure for human
review. Never silently accept the least-bad image.

## 9. Cross-page validation

After every scene passes individually:

- create a contact sheet in story order;
- compare the same character across all pages;
- compare every recurring object across all appearances;
- compare each setting as a continuity group;
- verify object states progress in the correct order;
- verify lighting and weather change only when specified;
- verify camera variety does not change the world's geometry;
- verify no page reveals a future event;
- verify art style, line weight and colour treatment are uniform.

Reject only the affected pages and repair them from locked references. Never
regenerate approved reference assets to fix page drift.

## 10. Deliverables

Use this structure:

```
output/images/[book_id]/
├── continuity.json
├── references/
│   ├── STYLE-01.png
│   ├── CHAR-01-full.png
│   ├── CHAR-01-face.png
│   ├── SET-01-master.png
│   └── OBJ-PLANT-01.png
├── scenes/
│   ├── cover.png
│   ├── page-01.png
│   └── page-02.png
├── qc/
│   ├── page-01.json
│   ├── page-02.json
│   └── contact-sheet.png
└── generation-log.json
```

Deliver only approved final scene images to the book assembler. Retain the
references, manifest and QC records so a single page can be regenerated later
without redesigning the book world.

## Engine notes

- **Current production engine (verified 2026-07):** Vertex AI, region
  `us-central1`, model `gemini-2.5-flash-image`, authenticated with the
  gcloud OAuth token (`gcloud auth print-access-token`) — the standalone
  Gemini API key is billing-depleted; never use it. Auth + call pattern:
  `scripts/regen_scene_vertex.py`. References go in as multiple labelled
  `inlineData` parts, `generationConfig.responseModalities: ["IMAGE"]`.
- Do NOT invent model IDs. `gemini-3.1-flash-image` and `gemini-3-pro-image`
  do not exist on this project. Before switching models, list what the
  project can actually call and run a regression book first.
- Prefer a current multi-reference model for new story scenes.
- Prefer a controlled image-edit model for small changes to an approved
  scene.
- Pin a tested model version in production and re-run a regression book
  before changing it.
- Keep engine configuration outside this skill in application config so
  model deprecations do not require rewriting the continuity workflow.
- Verify current official API schemas before changing endpoint names or
  request parameters.
- Do not lower safety controls to solve an ordinary generation failure.
- Legacy engines: Flux Kontext Pro via fal.ai (v1 pipeline) is retired for
  scene generation; FLUX Kontext remains an option for Mode B single-source
  controlled edits only. The v1 skill is archived at
  `SKILL_v1_flux_era.md.bak`.

---

## Field notes — first live run (L2.5 "The Yak and the Box", 2026-07-25)

Validated on Vertex `gemini-2.5-flash-image`, 16 generations, 7 scenes: 4
hard-pass, 3 flagged for human review. Contact sheet vs the old pipeline
shows dramatically better world consistency. Learnings now binding:

1. **Order references: previous approved scene FIRST**, labelled "THE MOST
   IMPORTANT REFERENCE … same illustrator, same book", then identity locks.
   With character refs first and no such framing, page 1 broke the entire
   world (new art style, realistic yak, white sclera, wrong hut).
2. **Name the proportions in words** ("chibi, large round head", "SHORT
   neck, head sits on the shoulders") — refs alone don't stop llama-neck /
   realism creep in action poses.
3. **Counting works when enumerated and unoccluded.** "EXACTLY SIX figs —
   count them: one, two, three in the front row…" succeeded on an isolated
   reference and in-scene once the lid was lifted clear. Never let the
   countable object be partially covered; if it is, change the staging, not
   the count.
4. **Colour repairs need hex + "the change must be obvious"** — a polite
   recolour request produced an imperceptible shift; `#8A6A50 (like milk
   chocolate), no grey tones left` worked.
5. **Never fix a wrong POSE/action with a controlled edit** — two failed
   attempts trying to make a standing yak sit. Follow the repair table:
   action mismatch = full regeneration with explicit scene geometry
   (left/right positions spelled out).
6. **Controlled edits can ADD content** (a repair injected extra flag
   strings and a second pole). Always re-run the full QC after any repair,
   and include "DO NOT add anything new" — it still isn't guaranteed.
7. **Version every candidate** — save `page-NN.attemptK.png` before
   overwriting; a bad repair destroyed a better prior candidate this run.
8. **Known drift hotspots to list in PRESERVE every time:** roof shape
   (flat→pitched on close-ups), flag strings (multiply, migrate, or vanish),
   scarf/clothing patterns (solid→striped), animal neck length, background
   building count.
9. **~4-5 refs per call worked**; style anchor can be dropped from scene
   calls once the previous approved scene carries the style.
10. **Aspect ratio:** `generationConfig.imageConfig.aspectRatio` is accepted
    by the Vertex endpoint — use it, plus full-bleed language ("fills the
    entire canvas edge to edge, no white frame/vignette").

---

## Appendix A: Character roster for template books

Each story should use a DIFFERENT character. Choose based on story context
(does the outfit suit the setting?), diversity across the collection, and
reader age. Note the choice in the story data file
(`"character_id": "CHAR-A"`).

House rules override roster text: ALL characters have solid black filled
oval/dot eyes with zero white, and NO blush or rosy cheeks — ignore any
"rosy cheeks" phrasing below.

### Indoor/Casual

- **CHAR-A: British-Asian girl (casual home)** — about 5, warm brown skin,
  long straight black hair in two low bunches; soft pink flower-print
  t-shirt, blue joggers, purple fluffy slippers. *Indoor, home, calm.*
- **CHAR-B: White British boy (casual home)** — about 5, light skin, short
  sandy brown hair, side parting, freckles; green striped t-shirt, navy
  joggers, cosy socks. *Indoor, home, playful.*
- **CHAR-C: Black British girl (smart casual)** — about 6, dark brown skin,
  afro puffs with colourful bobbles; yellow cardigan over white t-shirt,
  blue jeans, white canvas shoes. *Indoor/outdoor, school, shops.*

### Outdoor/Adventure

- **CHAR-D: British-Asian girl (outdoor adventure)** — about 5, dark brown
  skin, short curly black hair; bright red jumper, dark blue denim
  dungarees, blue wellies. *Gardens, muddy play, farms, puddles.*
- **CHAR-E: Mixed heritage boy (outdoor explorer)** — about 6, light brown
  skin, thick curly brown hair; orange waterproof jacket, dark green cargo
  trousers, brown hiking boots. *Nature walks, forest, park.*
- **CHAR-F: Muslim girl (rainy day)** — about 6, warm brown skin, soft
  lilac hijab; bright yellow raincoat over purple dress, red wellies.
  *Rainy days, autumn, weather themes.*

### Summer/Outdoor (modest)

- **CHAR-G: White British boy (summer outdoor)** — about 5, light skin,
  blonde hair, sun hat; light blue t-shirt, beige trousers, sandals, sun
  cream on nose. *Summer, park, garden.*
- **CHAR-H: South Asian girl (summer outdoor)** — about 6, medium brown
  skin, long black high ponytail; bright pink t-shirt, purple leggings,
  trainers. *Sports day, active play.*

### School/Formal

- **CHAR-I: Black British boy (school)** — about 7, dark brown skin, short
  black hair with neat shape-up; navy school jumper with white collar, grey
  trousers, black shoes. *School, learning, library.*
- **CHAR-J: East Asian girl (school)** — about 7, neat black bob with
  fringe; burgundy cardigan over white blouse, grey pleated skirt, white
  socks, black Mary Janes. *School, reading, quiet themes.*

### Pet owners

- **CHAR-K: White British girl (pet owner)** — about 6, light skin, long
  ginger single plait, freckles; teal hoodie, dark grey leggings, green
  trainers. *Pets, animals, caring themes.*
- **CHAR-L: Mixed heritage boy (pet owner)** — about 6, medium brown skin,
  messy dark brown curls; red hoodie, blue jeans, white trainers. *Pets,
  energetic, playful.*

Personalised books (uploaded_child mode) do NOT use this roster.

## Appendix B: Skin/hair hex table

Never use vague descriptions like "dark brown skin" — always specify hex plus
a plain-language name, and state relative ordering ("skin darker than X,
lighter than hair").

| Skin tone | Skin hex | Hair hex | Description |
|-----------|----------|----------|-------------|
| Very dark (West African) | `#3A2518` | `#0D0D0D` | Dark chocolate, near-black hair |
| Dark (East African) | `#4E3524` | `#0D0D0D` | Rich brown, black hair |
| Medium-dark (South Asian) | `#8B6B4A` | `#1A1A1A` | Warm brown, very dark hair |
| Medium (Mediterranean) | `#B8956A` | `#2C1810` | Golden brown, dark brown hair |
| Light-medium (East Asian) | `#D4A574` | `#1A1110` | Warm tan, near-black hair |
| Light (Northern European) | `#F0D0B0` | `#8B6B3D` | Peach, sandy brown hair |

If skin tone is wrong after generation, use a controlled recolour edit
("change ONLY the skin colour to [HEX] and hair to [HEX], keep everything
else EXACTLY the same") — do not regenerate.
